    23	`block-descent-a1-one-cusp-wild-valuation-structure-sol56-20260831.md`.
    24	
    25	## Executive verdict
    26	
    27	```text
    28	LEMMA A           CONFIRMED, with dependency note.
    29	SMOOTH-KILL       CONFIRMED.
    30	PROP 6.1          CONFIRMED.
    31	NODAL-ALL-N       CONFIRMED as a necessary numerical gate; no realization claim.
    32	THEOREM PROFILE   CONFIRMED, with B3 kept as an open residual.
    33	CUSP-KILL         CONFIRMED, conditional on the cited classical theorems.
    34	COR 7.2           CONFIRMED.
    35	N4-PIN            CONFIRMED.
    36	Controls          CONFIRMED: three-route (K), R_max spot checks, rank-four census.
    37	
    38	Coordinator claim "H2 complete at every degree"    REFUTED / overpromoted.
    39	```
    40	
    41	The mathematical classification in `MI` is not an all-degree closure of H2. It
    42	is an all-degree profile reduction with explicit survivors. This is already
    43	stated by `MI`: the H2 branch is "not closed at all degrees" (MI:39-44,
    44	751-758), with four typed OPENs in section 9. By contrast, `CI` says "Under H2
    45	... complete at every degree" (CI:67-73). That coordinator sentence must not be
    46	promoted from the reviewed material. The safe promotion is the profile theorem
    47	plus the typed residuals.
    48	
    49	## 1. LEMMA A and the old `[P5]` route
    50	
    51	**Verdict: CONFIRMED.**
    52	
    53	The old promoted route is genuinely vacuous after Theorem 7.B. The source
    54	`[P5]` theorem is conditional: if an affine-image dicritical has `mu_0=1`, then
    55	`s_0=1`, the dicritical cover is an isomorphism to the normalization, and
    56	`D~ ~= A^1` (`b0-all-n-eta-criticality`:175-192). Its proof uses the
    57	trivial-dicritical hypothesis explicitly at the differential step
    58	(`b0-all-n-eta-criticality`:127-150; review confirmation at
    59	`b0-all-n-hostile-review`:69-85). The coordinator's promoted Theorem 7.B says
    60	the opposite antecedent holds under H2: no affine-image dicritical has `mu=1`
    61	(CI:22-32). Therefore `[P5]` does not supply H3 in the H2/7.B regime. The
    62	vacuity warning in `MI` is real (MI:102-106, 148-156).
    63	
    64	Lemma A avoids that route. It uses Orevkov Lemma 2.1 to get, for every
    65	affine-image dicritical, `l ~= P^1` and `l' = l cap Phi^{-1}(A^2) ~= A^1`
    66	(MI:126-129). The local source/review files confirm this as degree-free and not
    67	dependent on H2 or H3 (`b0-all-n-hostile-review`:57-67;
    68	`n20-escape-hostile-review`:100-118). The map `Phi|_l : P^1 -> closure(D_l)` is
    69	a nonconstant morphism of projective curves, hence finite onto its image. Its
    70	restriction factors through the normalization as a finite surjection
    71	`h_l : A^1 -> D~_l` (MI:130-133).
    72	
    73	The rationality and one-place-at-infinity argument is sound. The finite
    74	surjection extends to projective completions, so the projective normalization
    75	of `D_l` is dominated by `P^1` and has genus zero. Since `D~_l` is affine and
    76	`h_l(A^1)` lies inside it, every point of the boundary
    77	`bar D~_l - D~_l` must be hit by the single point `infinity` of `P^1`; the
    78	boundary is nonempty and has exactly one point (MI:135-143). Therefore
    79	`D~_l ~= A^1`. Under H2, `A_F` has one component and it is the image of a
    80	dicritical, so H3 follows.
    81	
    82	Dependencies consumed: Orevkov Lemma 2.1, finite normalization of curves,
    83	Luroth/Riemann-Hurwitz-level rationality, and the standard fact that components
    84	of `A_F` arise as dicritical images. No use of 7.B, `[P5]`, `(M')`, or any
    85	unreviewed enumeration is hidden in the proof.
    86	
    87	## 2. SMOOTH-KILL
    88	
    89	**Verdict: CONFIRMED.**
    90	
    91	Assume `s=0`. Lemma A gives `D ~= A^1`, so `chi_c(D)=1` (MI:180-182). Because
    92	now `D_0=D`, the sheet-gate covering lemma applies to all of
    93	`E=F^{-1}(D)`, giving a degree-`a` finite topological/algebraic covering over
    94	`D`; hence `chi_c(E)=a chi_c(D)=a` (MI:184-185). Theorem (E) is available at
    95	this scope: it needs the Keller map to be proper and etale over
    96	`A^2-D`, plus compactly supported Euler additivity and finite-covering
    97	multiplicativity (`round1033-sheet-gate`:365-390;
    98	`round1033-sheet-gate-hostile-review`:189-197). No H3-free or smoothness
    99	mismatch is present. The equation gives `1=a` (MI:185-186).
   100	
   101	The two Abhyankar-Moh-Suzuki applications are also at their exact scope. First,
   102	`D` is a closed embedded copy of `A^1`, so a target automorphism rectifies it to
   103	`{v=0}` (MI:188-191). With `a=1`, `E -> D` is a degree-one etale cover, hence
   104	`E ~= A^1`; a source automorphism rectifies it to `{x=0}` (MI:191-193). Since
   105	`F` is etale, the pullback of the reduced divisor `{v=0}` is reduced. Its zero
   106	set is `{x=0}`, so `F_2=cx`, and the constant Jacobian forces
   107	`F_1=lambda y+g(x)`, an automorphism (MI:195-201). This contradicts the
   108	standing noninvertibility.
   109	
   110	No hypothesis mismatch was found in either challenged step: `chi_c(E)=a
   111	chi_c(D)` uses the covering lemma only after `D_0=D`, and Theorem (E) is a
   112	Keller theorem, not a theorem requiring smooth `A_F`.
   113	
   114	## 3. Local law, `(K)`, and Proposition 6.1
   115	
   116	**Verdict on Proposition 6.1: CONFIRMED.**
   117	
   118	The pointwise law
   119	
   120	```text
   121	N - a_p = r_p W + K_p,    K_p >= 0
   122	```
   123	
   124	is re-derived in `MI` (MI:222-246), with the only borrowed campaign step being
   125	the already-reviewed "no extra finite `L_C`-fibre points" clause (MI:237-240).
   126	At a smooth point this recovers `[P3]`, `N=a+W` (MI:248-249). Subtracting gives
   127	
   128	```text
   129	a - a_p = (r_p-1) W + K_p.                    (L)
   130	```
   131	
   132	Summing and comparing with `(M')` gives
   133	
   134	```text
   135	sum_p K_p = a - 1.                             (K)
   136	```
   137	
   138	The three advertised derivations agree. Route 1 is `(L)` plus `(M'-def)`
   139	(MI:253-258). Route 2 is Orevkov's
   140	`sum_l (mu_l+corr_l)=N-1` after substituting
   141	`corr_l=mu_l(s_l-1)+sum_t k_t` and using the polynomial-map Riemann-Hurwitz
   142	count (MI:258-262). Route 3 is the promoted `ALL-N (4.6)` identity (MI:256,
   143	763-766). Algebraically, Route 2 gives
   144	`W + sum_t k_t = N-1`; since `W=N-a` and the smooth-stratum terms have zero
   145	defect, this is exactly `sum_p K_p=a-1`.
   146	
   147	Proposition 6.1 is then immediate. If `s=1`, `(M'-def)` gives
   148	`a-a_p=(a-1)+(r_p-1)W`, so `a_p=1-(r_p-1)W`. With `W>=2` and `a_p>=0`, one must
   149	have `r_p=1` and `a_p=1` (MI:442-450). This is strictly weaker and cleaner than
   150	the old one-node exclusion: a single singular point is forced to be unibranch,
   151	not impossible.
   152	
   153	## 4. NODAL-ALL-N and enumeration
   154	
   155	**Verdict: CONFIRMED as a necessary numerical gate.**
   156	
   157	Under the stated hypothesis that every branch of `A_F` is smooth, a singular
   158	point cannot be unibranch; hence every singular point has `r_p>=2`, and
   159	`beta=0` (MI:340-344). From `(L)`, `a_p>=0` gives
   160	`r_p W <= N`; at multibranch points this implies `2a>=N` and
   161	`K_p <= D_gap := 2a-N` (MI:264-272, 342-345). From localization `(C3)`, at most
   162	`R=sum_l(s_l-1)` points have positive `K_p` (MI:291-295, 345). Combining with
   163	`(K)` gives
   164	
   165	```text
   166	a - 1 <= R D_gap.                              (5.3)
   167	```
   168	
   169	The 7.B input is exactly the bound on `R`: every `mu_l>=2`, so
   170	`sum_l s_l <= floor(W/2)`, and because `m>=1`,
   171	`R=sum_l(s_l-1)<=floor(W/2)-1` (MI:351-356). Thus the exact integer gate is
   172	
   173	```text
   174	a - 1 <= (2a-N) R_max(N-a),
   175	N/2 < a <= N-2,    N-a >= 2.                   (5.4)
   176	```
   177	
   178	I independently enumerated (5.4). The first survivors are exactly the table in
   179	`MI`:
   180	
   181	```text
   182	N=17: (a,W,D_gap,Rmax)=(11,6,5,2)
   183	N=18: (12,6,6,2)
   184	N=19: (13,6,7,2)
   185	N=20: (12,8,4,3), (14,6,8,2)
   186	N=21: (13,8,5,3), (15,6,9,2)
   187	N=22: (14,8,6,3), (16,6,10,2)
   188	N=23: (13,10,3,4), (15,8,7,3), (17,6,11,2)
   189	```
   190	
   191	The clean bound `R <= W/2-1` gives (5.5):
   192	
   193	```text
   194	N(D_gap-2) >= D_gap^2 + 6D_gap - 4.
   195	```
   196	
   197	For `D_gap=1,2` it is impossible; for `3,4,5,6,7,8` it gives thresholds
   198	`23,18,17,17,18,18`, and parity removes `D_gap=6` at `N=17` (MI:363-374).
   199	This proves no all-smooth-branch case below `N=17`.
   200	
   201	I also checked `R_max(W)` directly from its definition by dynamic programming
   202	over profiles `(s_i,mu_i)` with `s_i>=1`, `mu_i>=2`, and
   203	`sum s_i mu_i=W`:
   204	
   205	```text
   206	W=5:  Rmax=0, profile (1,2)+(1,3)
   207	W=6:  Rmax=2, profile (3,2)
   208	W=9:  Rmax=2, profile (3,2)+(1,3)
   209	W=15: Rmax=5, profile (6,2)+(1,3)
   210	```
   211	
   212	These match the formula in `MI`: even `W` gives `W/2-1`; odd `W` gives
   213	`max(W/p_min(W)-1,(W-5)/2)` (MI:354-356). No floor/attainment error was found:
   214	the table is a table of numerically admissible data, not a witness table.
   215	
   216	The `N<=7` part is genuinely 7.B-free. If 7.B is withdrawn and `mu=1` is
   217	allowed, the exact maximum becomes `R=W-1`; enumerating the weakened inequality
   218	has no solution for `N<=7` and first survives at
   219	`(N,a,W,D_gap,R)=(8,5,3,2,2)`, exactly as `MI` states (MI:396-399).
