   551	### 6.3 All degrees: where the crossing is, and how weak the bound may be
   552	
   553	B3E's G-ANTI table has no crossing because both of its ingredients move the same
   554	way when `k` grows. The crossing that does exist is between MERIDIAN-FLOOR and any
   555	upper bound:
   556	
   557	> **CROSSING (unconditional).** The cell `(N, W)` is EMPTY as soon as one proves
   558	> `n_min <= C` with `C < ceil((N-1)/(W-1))`.
   559	> **CROSSING (delta form, conditional on OPEN[MIN-EMBED-DEGREE]).** The cell is
   560	> EMPTY as soon as one proves `delta_aff <= C` with `C < ceil(ceil((N-1)/(W-1))/3)`
   561	> — the second entry of the §4 table.
   562	
   563	Read off the table: at `W = 2` a bound `n_min <= C` with `C < N-1` kills the whole
   564	column, at every `N`; in `delta_aff` form, `C < 4` kills `W = 2` at `N = 11..13`,
   565	`C < 5` at `N = 14..16`, `C < 7` at `N = 20`. Compare with the `(B2)` route of
   566	§6.1, which demands `delta_aff <= 1` at `11 <= N <= 16`: **at `W = 2` the crossing
   567	route needs a strictly weaker bound than the `beta` route** (`C <= 3` vs `C <= 1`
   568	at `N = 11`), and it applies to `(B3)` as well, where the `beta` route does not.
   569	This is the concrete payoff of MERIDIAN-FLOOR: it lowers the price of the still-open
   570	input.
   571	
   572	**Why the covering cap cannot supply the other half.** B3E's `2g(E) <= 1 - a +
   573	sum_p r_p max(0,(r_p-1)W + K_p - 1)` is an upper bound on `g(E)`; on the `(B3)`
   574	profile with `k` double points it reads `2g(E) <= 2k(W-1) + K_c - 1 - a + 1`,
